WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.600
Every action potential lets potassium out of the neuron.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:13.360
When a small patch of brain fires hard, a lot of potassium leaks out.

3
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:16.820
The space between brain cells is narrow.

4
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.200
So the potassium can build up there.

5
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.860
A neuron's resting voltage depends largely on potassium.

6
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.020
Something has to put it back.

7
00:00:26.883 --> 00:00:32.623
Take an illustrative neuron with 140 millimolar potassium inside.

8
00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:37.083
At rest, 3 millimolar potassium sits outside.

9
00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:42.003
EK is then minus 102.6 millivolts.

10
00:00:42.823 --> 00:00:47.583
After intense firing, suppose a small space reaches 12 millimolar.

11
00:00:48.283 --> 00:00:51.843
EK becomes minus 65.6 millivolts.

12
00:00:52.323 --> 00:00:55.143
That is 37 millivolts more positive.

13
00:00:55.143 --> 00:01:01.763
A real neuron's resting voltage moves less, but in the same direction, toward threshold.

14
00:01:02.217 --> 00:01:06.897
Astrocytes, a kind of glial cell, do a large share of that work.

15
00:01:07.617 --> 00:01:09.457
They use spatial buffering.

16
00:01:10.297 --> 00:01:14.477
Potassium enters an astrocyte where the outside concentration is high.

17
00:01:15.197 --> 00:01:19.857
It travels through that astrocyte, and through the network of astrocytes joined to

18
00:01:19.857 --> 00:01:20.957
it by gap junctions.

19
00:01:21.617 --> 00:01:23.877
It leaves where the concentration is low.

20
00:01:25.057 --> 00:01:29.457
Think of a sponge that soaks up a spill at one end and wrings out at the other.

21
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:37.560
The entry channel is largely Kir4.1, an inwardly rectifying potassium channel.

22
00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:44.500
Separately, the sodium potassium ATPase brings potassium into glia for net uptake.

23
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.700
Redistribution and net uptake are distinct mechanisms.

24
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:53.473
Ken McCarthy's laboratory tested the channel with genetics.

25
00:01:54.333 --> 00:01:58.213
They used a conditional knockout driven by a glial promoter.

26
00:01:59.373 --> 00:02:03.613
Djukic and colleagues deleted Kir4.1 from glia.

27
00:02:04.473 --> 00:02:11.173
In hippocampal slices from mice 15 to 20 days old, CA1 astrocytes took up potassium

28
00:02:11.173 --> 00:02:16.953
poorly. The deletion began in development, and it also reached oligodendrocytes.

29
00:02:16.953 --> 00:02:20.813
So the result cannot be pinned on mature astrocytes alone.

30
00:02:21.667 --> 00:02:29.167
Keep this rule. When neurons fire hard, potassium can build up outside them, and EK

31
00:02:29.167 --> 00:02:30.627
becomes more positive.

32
00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:36.647
The real resting voltage moves the same way, toward threshold, but less.

33
00:02:38.167 --> 00:02:42.787
Astrocytes pass that potassium through their network, from where it is high to where

34
00:02:42.787 --> 00:02:45.347
it is low. That is redistribution.

35
00:02:46.227 --> 00:02:51.267
Net uptake by the sodium-potassium ATPase is a separate mechanism.
